---
// How routes are checked: the short, public version of docs/ROLES.md. Every
// number on this page is counted from the content at build time.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { content } from '../lib/site';

const c = content();
const routes = c.roles.filter((r) => !r.variantOf);
const jobRoutes = routes.filter((r) => r.kind !== 'exam');
const checked = jobRoutes.filter((r) => r.evidence);
const drafts = jobRoutes.filter((r) => !r.evidence);
const listings = checked.reduce((n, r) => n + r.evidence!.postings, 0);
const months = [...new Set(checked.map((r) => r.evidence!.collected))].sort();
const monthName = (ym: string) => new Date(`${ym}-01T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const sections = c.concepts.flatMap((x) => x.resources);
const exams = routes.filter((r) => r.kind === 'exam');
const REPO = 'https://github.com/shivemn/terminus';
---

<Base title="How routes are checked" description="Where Terminus's routes come from, how they're checked against real job listings, and how the links are kept honest.">
  <article class="method">
    <h1><span class="board">How routes are checked</span></h1>
    <p class="lede quiet">
      A route is only as good as its hiring list. Here is where each list comes from, what "checked" means, and what we
      do to keep it true.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Every role starts as a draft</h2>
      <p>
        Each role lists what it needs in three tiers: what gets a fresher hired, what the first year on the job expects,
        and what's good to have. A first list is our own judgement, and the role page says so: it's marked "Draft".
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Then it's checked against real listings</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>We collect real entry-level postings for the role, usually around 50, for India and 0 to 2 years of experience. They're copied by hand from job sites such as Naukri and LinkedIn and from company career pages. Nothing is scraped.</li>
        <li>Each posting is tagged with the topics it asks for, and saved as one row in a spreadsheet in the repository.</li>
        <li>A script counts how often each topic is asked for. Asked for by at least half the postings suggests "to get hired", a fifth to a half suggests "first year", and less than that "good to have".</li>
        <li>A person decides. Postings under-report some things: almost none say "data structures and algorithms", yet nearly every software interview in India tests them. When we keep something against the numbers, the role says why.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Once that's done, the role page says how many listings it was checked against and when, with a link to the
        spreadsheet. So far {checked.length} of {jobRoutes.length} job routes have been checked, against
        {' '}{listings.toLocaleString('en-IN')} listings collected in {months.map(monthName).join(' and ')}. The other {drafts.length} still say "Draft".
        {exams.length > 0 && <> The {exams.length} exams follow their official syllabus instead.</>}
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Every resource is opened and checked</h2>
      <p>
        Each stop points to the chapters, lectures or exercises that teach it, never just a whole book. All
        {' '}{sections.length.toLocaleString('en-IN')} of those references have been opened by a person and the chapter or lecture
        checked; the site won't build if one hasn't. Every pick is free. The few paid books that are worth knowing about
        are listed as alternatives, and marked as paid.
      </p>
      <p>A check runs over every link on the first of each month, and opens an issue for anything broken or moved.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Kept up to date</h2>
      <p>
        Job markets move, so the checked routes are collected again every year. Every November a reminder opens on
        GitHub with the list of checked routes and the drafts still waiting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full notes, the spreadsheets and the scripts are all in the
        {' '}<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/ROLES.md`}>repository</a>. Spotted something wrong, or know a better
        resource? <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>Tell us on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a class="btn" href="/#start">Plan my route <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .method { display: grid; gap: 34px; }
  .method h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); }
  .lede { font-size: 1.15rem; max-width: 60ch; }
  section { display: grid; gap: 12px; padding-top: 22px; border-top: 2px solid var(--ink); }
  h2 { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.1rem); font-weight: 700; }
  ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.4em; display: grid; gap: 10px; }
  ol li::marker { font-family: var(--sign); font-weight: 700; }
  .back { margin-top: 6px; }
</style>
